  <main>
    <section class="card span12">
      <p class="proof-note"><b>Testing, not proof.</b> The rules below were fixed before the results came in, so they can't be tuned
        to make a pattern appear. A match only means two things happened within 48 hours of each other.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card span12">
      <h2>Solar wind spikes</h2>
      <p class="note">Source: NOAA SWPC real-time solar wind, 1-minute proton speed from whichever spacecraft is active. It's reduced to hourly medians,
        and hours with fewer than 20 good minutes are skipped. The record before this logger started (Nov 18, 2026 onward) comes from the RealAether
        Cosmic Ray Detector, which logs the same feed.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Spike rule:</b> an hour whose median speed is at least <b>100 km/s</b> above the median of the hours <b>30 to 6 hours</b> before it
        (a 24-hour baseline ending 6 hours earlier), or an hour that crosses up through <b>500 km/s</b>. A new spike needs <b>12 hours</b> since the previous spike start.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card span12">
      <h2>Volcano alert changes</h2>
      <p class="note">Source: USGS Hazard Notification System (HANS). Each Volcano Activity Notice or VONA is read for its current and previous
        Alert Level and Aviation Color Code, and logged once if either changed. A snapshot of all monitored U.S. volcanoes is compared run to run as a safety net.
        The time used is the notice's sent time.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card span12">
      <h2>Matches and chance</h2>
      <p class="note">An alert change is a <b>match</b> if a spike start falls within <b>±48 hours</b> of it. Only changes inside the solar wind record are tested.
        Changes from before the record are listed but not counted.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Chance:</b> the share of the record that sits within ±48 h of any spike is the probability that a randomly timed alert change would match.
        Expected matches = tested changes × that share. The p-value is a one-sided binomial tail. Alert changes at the same volcano are not independent,
        so treat the p-value as rough. Only a long record that beats chance by a wide margin would be worth a closer look, and even that would not show cause.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card span12">
      <h2>Schedule and data</h2>
      <p class="note">The logger runs every 30 minutes at the lowest priority on a Raspberry Pi 5 and publishes static files only.
        Raw data (CC BY 4.0, credit "RealAether (Geebs369)"): <a href="data/wind_hourly.csv">wind_hourly.csv</a> · <a href="data/spikes.csv">spikes.csv</a> ·
